INSERT OR REPLACE INTO cms_items (c_id, i_id, title, pic, content, addtime) VALUES (66, 189, '4.0-4.5Microns BOPET TTR (Thermal Transfer Ribbons) basic film series for carbon', '../upload/image/20180922/20180922162954_68886.jpg', '<p></p><p><span style="color:#009900;font-family:Verdana;font-size:16px;"><strong>4.0-4.5Microns BOPET TTR (Thermal Transfer Ribbons) basic film series for carbon</strong></span></p> <p><span style="color:#009900;font-family:Verdana;font-size:16px;"><br/> </span></p> <p><span style="color:#009900;font-family:Verdana;font-size:16px;"><span style="font-size:16px;color:#009900;"><strong>What is BOPET TTR Film?</strong></span><br/> <span style="font-size:16px;color:#009900;"><strong>BOPET stands for Biaxially Oriented Polyethylene Terephthalate — a type of polyester (PET) film that has been stretched in two directions (machine direction and transverse direction) to enhance its mechanical and thermal properties. When used as the base film for Thermal Transfer Ribbons (TTR), it serves as the substrate onto which ink/wax/resin coatings are applied for thermal printing.</strong></span><br/> <span style="font-size:16px;color:#009900;"><strong>Polyester film is the base material of choice for Thermal Transfer Ribbons (TTR). The thermal transfer printing applications are segmented into "standard TTR," Dye Diffusion Thermal Transfer (D2T2), and thermal stencil. For standard TTR, the main thickness is 4.5 microns. </strong></span><br/> <br/> <span style="font-size:16px;color:#009900;"><strong>Key Specifications</strong></span><br/> <span style="font-size:16px;color:#009900;"><strong>Available thickness options for BOPET thermal transfer film include 4.4, 4.5, 4.55, 4.6, 4.8, 5.0, 5.7, and 6.0 µm. </strong></span></span></p> <p><span style="color:#009900;font-family:Verdana;font-size:16px;"><span style="font-size:16px;color:#009900;"><strong></strong></span><span style="font-size:16px;color:#009900;"><strong>Common widths include 500, 600, 910, 980, 1010, 1200, and 1500 mm, and roll lengths range from 15,000 to 37,000 meters. Ec21</strong></span><br/> <span style="font-size:16px;color:#009900;"><strong>Property Detail Material BOPET (Biaxially Oriented PET)</strong></span></span></p> <p><span style="color:#009900;font-family:Verdana;font-size:16px;"><span style="color:#009900;font-family:Verdana;font-size:16px;"><strong>Typical Specifications </strong></span><br/> <span style="color:#009900;font-family:Verdana;font-size:16px;"><strong>Property Typical Value</strong></span><br/> <span style="color:#009900;font-family:Verdana;font-size:16px;"><strong>Thickness 4.5 μm (±0.1 μm)</strong></span><br/> <span style="color:#009900;font-family:Verdana;font-size:16px;"><strong>Width300–2080 mm (jumbo rolls)</strong></span><br/> <span style="color:#009900;font-family:Verdana;font-size:16px;"><strong>LengthUp to 36,000+ meters per roll</strong></span><br/> <span style="color:#009900;font-family:Verdana;font-size:16px;"><strong>SurfacePlain (often no corona) or treated for adhesion</strong></span><br/> <span style="color:#009900;font-family:Verdana;font-size:16px;"><strong>Core Diameter152.4 mm (6 inches)</strong></span><br/> <span style="color:#009900;font-family:Verdana;font-size:16px;"><strong>Outer Roll Diameter350–600 mm</strong></span><br/> <span style="color:#009900;font-family:Verdana;font-size:16px;"><strong>Heat Shrinkage≤1.5% (150°C, 30 min)</strong></span><span style="font-size:16px;color:#009900;"><strong></strong></span><br/> </span></p> <p><span style="color:#009900;font-family:Verdana;font-size:16px;"><strong>Appearance: Clear / Transparent</strong></span></p> <p><span style="color:#009900;font-family:Verdana;font-size:16px;"><span style="font-size:16px;color:#009900;"><strong>Surface: Unprinted &amp; uncoated (base film) </strong></span><span style="font-size:16px;color:#009900;"><strong>HS Code3920620000</strong></span></span></p> <p><span style="color:#009900;font-family:Verdana;font-size:16px;"><span style="font-size:16px;color:#009900;"><strong>4.5Microns Biaxially Oriented Polyethylene Terephthalate (BOPET)</strong></span><br/> <span style="font-size:16px;color:#009900;"><strong>Flexible Packaging: Metallized or plain versions for food packaging, labels, and lamination (provides barrier and printability).</strong></span><br/> <span style="font-size:16px;color:#009900;"><strong>Printing &amp; Laminating: High-quality printing base for graphics and composites.</strong></span><br/> </span></p> <div> <br/> </div> <span style="font-size:16px;color:#009900;"><strong>Key Features &amp; Performance</strong></span><br/> <span style="font-size:16px;color:#009900;"><strong>The combination of mechanical strength and thermal resistance makes polyester film unique for the TTR market. Key properties include dimensional stability, ease of processing/coating, heat resistance, and tear resistance. M-petfilm</strong></span><br/> <span style="font-size:16px;color:#009900;"><strong>BOPET thermal transfer ribbons have excellent stability features in shrinkage and thickness consistency, good heat resistance, and are easy to process in thermal transfer applications. Boppfilmsales</strong></span><br/> <span style="font-size:16px;color:#009900;"><strong>Additionally, compared to transitional capacitor-grade films previously used in TTR applications, purpose-built BOPET for TTR shows greatly improved heat stability, stripping resistance, cleanliness, and surface adaptability to coated solvents, meeting the various performance requirements needed for high-speed TTR coating. </strong></span><br/> <br/> <span style="font-size:16px;color:#009900;"><strong>Applications</strong></span><br/> <span style="font-size:16px;color:#009900;"><strong>TTR films are used in a variety of applications including bar code printers, fax printers, portable printers, ticketing machines, monochrome ribbons, and color ribbons. M-petfilm</strong></span><br/> <span style="font-size:16px;color:#009900;"><strong>They are also used as the base for hot stamping films and silver metallized films in specialty printing.</strong></span><br/> <br/> <span style="font-size:16px;color:#009900;"><strong>Why 4.5 Microns?</strong></span><br/> <span style="font-size:16px;color:#009900;"><strong>The 4.5-micron gauge represents the industry sweet spot for standard TTR:</strong></span><br/> <br/> <span style="font-size:16px;color:#009900;"><strong>Thin enough to transfer heat quickly and efficiently from the print head to the ink layer</strong></span><br/> <span style="font-size:16px;color:#009900;"><strong>Strong enough to resist tearing during high-speed processing and printing</strong></span><br/> <span style="font-size:16px;color:#009900;"><strong>Dimensionally stable to prevent warping, shrinking, or stretching during coating and ribbon slitting</strong></span><br/> <br/> <span style="font-size:16px;color:#009900;"><strong>Certain applications use 5.7-micron film, and some specialty products are based on film thicknesses below 4.5 microns — but 4.5 µm is the dominant commercial standard. </strong></span><br/> <br/> <span style="font-size:16px;color:#009900;"><strong>Typical Roll Dimensions (Commercial Supply)</strong></span><br/> <span style="font-size:16px;color:#009900;"><strong>Roll lengths available include 15,000; 16,300; 17,000; 18,500; 20,000; 24,000; 36,000; 40,000; 44,000; and up to 55,000 meters. Common widths include 1,200; 1,290; 1,350; 1,400; 1,500; and 1,560 mm. Longer rolls reduce the number of joints in downstream processing, which is critical for high-speed ribbon coating lines. </strong></span><br/> <br/> <span style="font-size:16px;color:#009900;"><strong>Summary</strong></span><br/> <span style="font-size:16px;color:#009900;"><strong>BOPET TTR Film at 4.5 microns is a highly engineered, ultra-thin polyester film designed specifically as the backbone of thermal transfer printing ribbons. It balances mechanical durability, thermal conductivity, dimensional stability, and surface compatibility — making it the global standard base film for barcode, label, fax, and specialty thermal printing industries.</strong></span> <p><br/></p><p><span style="color:#009900;font-family:Verdana;font-size:16px;"><strong>BOPET TTR film (polyester film for transfer print) were basic film for BOPET Thermal Transfer color Ribbons film,HOT STAMPING FILM</strong></span><br/> <span style="color:#009900;font-family:Verdana;font-size:16px;"><strong> Thickness of BOPET thermal transfer film:4.0,4.2,4.3,4.4,4.5,4.55,4.6,4.8,5.0,5.7,6.0um </strong></span><br/> <span style="color:#009900;font-family:Verdana;font-size:16px;"><strong> width of BOPET thermal transfer basic film: 500,600,910,980,1010,1200,1500mm</strong></span><br/> <span style="color:#009900;font-family:Verdana;font-size:16px;"><strong> length of BOPET thermal transfer basic film:15000,17000,20000,24000,37000m </strong></span><br/> <span style="color:#009900;font-family:Verdana;font-size:16px;"><strong> feature of BOPET thermal transfer basic film: clear/transparent </strong></span><br/> <span style="color:#009900;font-family:Verdana;font-size:16px;"><strong> Thermal Transfer Ribbons have stability features in shrinkage and thickness stability</strong></span><br/> <span style="color:#009900;font-family:Verdana;font-size:16px;"><strong> ,in good heat resistance,easy processing in thermal transfer</strong></span><br/> <span style="color:#009900;font-family:Verdana;font-size:16px;"><strong> Application of BOPET thermal transfer basic film:suitable for use in bar code printers,fax machines and transfer ribbon. polyester film for transfer print</strong></span></p> <p><br/></p>', '2026-09-06 20:58:45');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO cms_items (c_id, i_id, title, pic, content, addtime) VALUES (66, 259, 'B grade stock BOPET film 3.8-4.55MIC uncoating , clear transparent', '../upload/image/20180922/20180922163031_82226.jpg', '<p></p><p style="text-align:left;color:#3A3A3A;text-indent:0px;background-color:#FFFFFF;"> <span arial="" black="" style="color: rgb(0, 153, 0); font-family: Verdana;"><span style="color:#009900;font-family:Verdana;background-color:#FFFFFF;"><strong>4.5 micrometer BOPET substandard products for use in printing composites &amp; lamination <br/> 4.5µm BOPET substandard (off-grade) products in the printing and lamination<br/> B grade stock BOPET TTR film 3.8-4.55MIC uncoating , clear transparent</strong></span></span></p> <p style="text-align:left;color:#3A3A3A;text-indent:0px;background-color:#FFFFFF;"> <span arial="" black="" style="color: rgb(0, 153, 0); font-family: Verdana;"><span style="color:#009900;font-family:Verdana;background-color:#FFFFFF;"><strong><br/> In the world of Biaxially-Oriented Polyethylene Terephthalate (BOPET) production, 4.5-micrometer (4.5MIC) film is an ultra-thin specialty product primarily designed for high-end applications like Thermal Transfer Ribbons (TTR) <br/> "Substandard" (Off-grade/B-grade) refers to batches that fail the rigorous "Electrical" or "TTR" grade standards due to minor thickness fluctuations, surface crystals (pinholes), or tension inconsistencies. However, these materials are highly sought after in the Printing Composite (Lamination) industry due to their extreme cost-efficiency.<br/> 1. Why Use Substandard 4.5μm Film for Composites? In standard flexible packaging, 12micPET is the norm. <br/> Shifting to 4.5MICsubstandard products offers three major strategic advantages:<br/> Extreme High Yield: 4.5MIC film provides approximately 2.6 times more surface area per kilogram compared to 12mic film (158M2 vs 60 m^<br/> 2.Cost Reduction: Substandard products are often sold at a 20%–40% discount compared to prime TTR-grade film. Combined with the high yield, this drastically slashes raw material costs.<br/> Enhanced Barrier Properties: Even as a "substandard" product, PET offers superior oxygen and aroma barriers compared to BOPP or PE of the same thickness.<br/> 2. Typical Application ScenariosSubstandard 4.5mic film is rarely used alone; it is almost always laminated to a thicker substrate which provides the necessary mechanical support.A. Paper-to-Film Lamination (Luxury Packaging)<br/> Application: Gift boxes, high-end shopping bags, and book covers.Process: The film is laminated to cardboard. The paper provides the "backbone," while the 4.5mic PET adds a high-gloss, water-resistant finish.<br/> Benefit: Minor thickness variations in the film are invisible once bonded to the opaque paper.<br/> B. Vacuum Metallized Composites (VMPET)Application: Snack food pouches (inner layer) or decorative "gold/silver" cardstock.Process: <br/> The film is metallized with aluminum. Small surface defects (crystals) are often covered by the metal layer.<br/> Benefit: Provides an excellent moisture barrier for a fraction of the cost of standard VMPET.<br/> C. Triple-Layer Flexible Packaging Structure: BOPP / 4.5 mic PET Substandard} / CPP.<br/> Usage: Used as a middle "stiffening" and barrier layer. It adds "crinkle" and heat resistance to the pouch without adding significant bulk.<br/> 3. Technical Challenges &amp; WorkaroundsUsing substandard ultra-thin film requires specific adjustments on the factory floor to avoid high scrap rates:<br/> 4. When dealing with 4.5µm BOPET substandard (off-grade) products in the printing and lamination sector, your communication needs to pivot from "precision/perfection" to "yield/value/utility." <br/> Here are the essential keywords categorized for marketing, technical negotiation, and procurement.<br/> 1. Value Proposition Keywords (The "Sales" Pitch)Since these are substandard products, the focus is on the economic benefit for the lamination plant.<br/> Yield Advantage: The most important term. It refers to the massive amount of square meters obtained per kilogram (158 m^2).<br/> Cost-Benefit Ratio: Highlighting that the lower price point justifies the minor technical adjustments needed.Substrate Replacement: Using 4.5µm to replace thicker $10\mu\text{m}$ or $12\mu\text{m}$ films in non-critical applications.Plastic Reduction / Lightweighting: Positioning the film as a "Green" solution by using less polymer for the same coverage area.<br/> Overrun / Stock Lot: Professional terms for "substandard" that imply the product is still high quality but available at a clearance price.<br/> 2. Technical Deficiency Keywords (The "Honest" Jargon)To manage customer expectations and avoid claims, use these terms to describe why it is substandard.<br/> Gauge Variation / Thickness Fluctuation: The film is not perfectly 4.5mic across the entire web.<br/> Surface Defects / Gel / Fish-eyes: Small lumps or impurities in the film that might affect high-definition printing but are fine for lamination.<br/> Tension Inconsistency: The roll might be "loose" on one side and "tight" on the other (baggy edges).<br/> Profile Deviation: Refers to the "high and low" spots on the jumbo roll.<br/> Transmittance Issues: If the film is slightly hazy or not perfectly clear.<br/> 3. Lamination &amp; Processing Keywords<br/> These terms describe how the film will be used in the factory.<br/> Dry Lamination: The most common process for this film (using solvent-based or solvent-less adhesives)<br/> .Paper-to-Film Bonding: Specifically for greeting cards, book covers, and luxury boxes.<br/> Tie-Layer: Using the PET as a middle layer between two other materials (e.g., BOPP/PET/PE).<br/> Corona Treatment / Dyne Level: Ensuring the "off-grade" film still has enough surface energy for the glue to stick.<br/> Low-Tension Unwinding: A necessary machine setting to handle ultra-thin substandard film without snapping it.<br/> 4. Application-Specific KeywordsWhere exactly is this "substandard" film going Gold/Silver <br/> Cardstock: Laminated PET used as a base for metallic inks in the tobacco or liquor packaging industry.<br/> Thermal Lamination: If the PET has been pre-coated with EVA glue (though rare for 4.5µm, it’s a growing niche).<br/> Flexible Packaging Inner Layer: Used for its barrier properties in snack food or condiment pouches.<br/> Protective Over-lamination: Simply providing a "scratch-proof" layer for printed paper.<br/> 5. Logistics &amp; Specification Keywords Crucial for the "Proforma Invoice" or "Packing List."<br/> Jumbo Rolls: Usually 24,000m to 40,000m in length.<br/> Net Weight vs. Gross Weight: Standard trading terms.<br/> Core Diameter: Usually 3" or 6".<br/> COF (Coefficient of Friction): Very important; if the COF is too high, the "substandard" film will stick to itself and be unusable.<br/> OD (Outer Diameter): Ensuring the roll fits on the customer''s lamination machine.</strong></span></span></p> <p style="text-align:left;color:#3A3A3A;text-indent:0px;background-color:#FFFFFF;"> <span arial="" black="" style="color: rgb(0, 153, 0); font-family: Verdana;"><span style="color:#009900;font-family:Verdana;background-color:#FFFFFF;"><strong>6. Sizes for B grade stock BOPET TTR film 3.8-4.55MIC uncoating , clear transparent<br/> Thcikness: 3.5,3.8,4.0, 4.1,4.2,4.3,4.4,4.5,4.55,4.6,4.8, 5.0 ,5.3, 6.0um<br/> width of BOPET thermal transfer basic film: 360,500,620,685,650,700,750,850,900,905,910,980,1010,1020,1030,1090,<br/> 1200,1290,1350,1400,1500,1560mm<br/> length of BOPET thermal transfer basic film:15000,16300,17000,18500,20000,24000,36000.40000,44000,55000m<br/> feature of BOPET thermal transfer basic film: clear/transparent<br/> Usage: for printing,and laminating, and silver metallized<br/> Thermal Transfer Ribbons have stability features in shrinkage and thickness stability<br/> ,in good heat resistance,easy processing in thermal transfer<br/> Application of BOPET thermal transfer basic film:suitable for use in bar code printers,fax machines and<br/> transfer ribbon. polyester film for transfer print<br/> Some size for reference:<br/> 1.) 4.5Mic(T) x 1010mm(W) x 18,000M(L) x 6" (inner paper tube core).<br/> 2.) 4.5Mic(T) x 910mm (W) x 18,000 meters (L), Core : 152mm ID x 700mm L<br/> 3.) 4.5Mic(T) x 620mm (W) x 18,000 meters (L), Core : 152mm ID x 700mm L<br/> Output: 2200 – 2500 ton/ month<br/> <br/> <div> <br/> </div> </strong></span></span></p>', '2026-09-06 20:58:45');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO cms_items (c_id, i_id, title, pic, content, addtime) VALUES (66, 261, 'BOPET polyester film 4.2-4.5microns', '../upload/image/20180922/20180922163359_98168.jpg', '<p></p><h3 style=''text-align:left;color:red;text-indent:0px;font-family:"font-style:normal;background-color:#FFFFFF;''> <span style="color:#009900;font-family:Verdana;font-size:16px;"><strong>4.5Microns BOPET thermal transfer film<br/> <br/> 4.5-Micron (4.5μm) BOPET (Biaxially-Oriented Polyethylene Terephthalate) film is the "gold standard" base material for the global Thermal Transfer Ribbon (TTR) industry. At this thickness, the film achieves a critical balance between thermal conductivity, mechanical strength, and cost-efficiency.<br/> Below is a comprehensive technical and industrial breakdown of this specialized material.<br/> 1. Technical Specifications &amp; Material Structure<br/> BOPET is produced by stretching polyethylene terephthalate in two perpendicular directions (Machine Direction - MD, and Transverse Direction - TD). For TTR applications, the film is not just "thin plastic"; it is a high-performance carrier.<br/> Core Physical Properties<br/> 2. The Multi-Layer Architecture of TTR<br/> In a finished thermal transfer ribbon, the 4.5-micron BOPET acts as the central skeleton. A high-quality ribbon consists of four distinct layers:<br/> Back-coat Layer (&lt;0.1μm): Protects the print head from friction and prevents static build-up.<br/> BOPET Base Film (4.5μm): The carrier that withstands the heat and tension.<br/> Release/Primer Layer: Ensures the ink peels off cleanly when heated.<br/> Ink Layer (2−4μm): Wax, Wax-Resin, or Resin that transfers to the label.<br/> 3. Why 4.5 Microns? (The Industry Choice)<br/> While TTR can range from 3.5μm to 6.0μm, the 4.5-micron specification dominates for several reasons:<br/> Thermal Sensitivity: It is thin enough to allow near-instantaneous heat transfer from the print head to the ink, enabling high-speed printing (up to 12 inches per second).<br/> Roll Capacity: Standard industrial printers have fixed outer diameter limits for ribbon rolls. A 4.5-micron base allows for longer rolls (e.g., 450m or 600m), reducing downtime for roll changes.<br/> Cost vs. Performance: It is the "sweet spot" where the film is thin enough to save on material costs but thick enough to maintain the structural integrity needed for high-speed coating machines.<br/> 4. Key Application Segments<br/> Wax Ribbons: The most common use. 4.5-micron film is the standard carrier for shipping labels and retail tags.<br/> Wax-Resin Ribbons: Used for higher-durability labels (e.g., pharmaceutical or warehouse tracking).<br/> Resin Ribbons: Usually requires the highest grade of 4.5-micron film with superior heat resistance for chemical and automotive labeling.<br/> 5. Global Market Dynamics<br/> Challenges in Production 4.5-micron TTR base film.<br/> Manufacturing 4.5-micron film is significantly harder than standard packaging film (12μm). The main hurdles are:<br/> Thickness Uniformity: Even a 0.3μm deviation can cause "dark spots" or "light spots" in the final print.<br/> Dust Control: In a film this thin, a single microscopic dust particle can cause a "pinhole" or a tear during the coating process.<br/> 6. Future Trends<br/> Down-gauging: The industry is slowly testing 4.0μm and 3.5μm films to fit more meters on a roll, though this requires significantly more expensive resin and tighter machine tolerances.<br/> Sustainability: Development of "Post-Consumer Recycled" (PCR) BOPET for ribbons to meet ESG goals of major retailers.<br/> 7. the key words Of 4.5Microns BOPET film --<br/> 4.5-Micron (4.5MIC) BOPET film TTR Base Film: (Thermal Transfer Ribbon Base Film) — The most specific industry term.<br/> Ultra-thin BOPET: Differentiates the product from standard 12mic packaging film<br/> High-Heat PET Film: Highlights the film''s ability to withstand print head temperatures.<br/> Capacitor Grade Film: (Sometimes used interchangeably due to similar thickness, <br/> though TTR grade has different surface treatments).4.5µm Polyester Carrier: Emphasizes its role as a vehicle for ink.<br/> 8. Technology<br/> BOPET polyester film 4.2-4.5microns<br/> BOPET Film for Thermal Transfer Ribbons type TTR<br/> 1、 Characteristics<br/> It is stretched in biaxial orientation by mean of flat film method by using<br/> imported polyethylene terephthalate in electrical grade. With good mechanical<br/> properties, and thermal characteristics, it has excellent engineering properties<br/> in coating process.<br/> 2、 Applications<br/> As a kind of substrate, it is widely applied to a variety of bar code printing,<br/> advertising banner printing, label printing, photo printing and other industries.<br/> 3、 Specifications<br/> Size(μm) WMV(μm) MMV(μm) Test method GB/T<br/> 4.0 4.0±0.1 4.2±4% 13542.2-2009/4<br/> 4.3 4.3±0.1 4.5±4%<br/> 4.5 4.5±0.1 47±4%<br/> Width of the finished film roll mm<br/> 700±2, 910±2,1010±2 oras customer’s requirement<br/> 700±2,910±2,1010±2<br/> Inner diameter （D0） mm Φ152.4<br/> picture Outer diameter （D） mm 350-550<br/> Note: For special specifications, please discuss with us separately.<br/> 4、Thickness Representation<br/> The nominal value of the film thickness is indicated by the central value<br/> (integer value, such as 3,5,7,10,15) of film thickness plus mantissa (one point after<br/> decimal point) or its code. Our nominal value for BOPET film thickness is representedin WMV.<br/> <br/> <br/> </strong></span> </h3>', '2026-09-06 20:58:45');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO cms_items (c_id, i_id, title, pic, content, addtime) VALUES (66, 360, '4.5Mic BOPET film clear transparent , unprinting &  uncoating ( for priting and laminating) ,', '../upload/image/20190103/20190103194539_80875.jpg', '<p><span style="color:#FF0000;background-color:rgba(0, 0, 0, 0);"><span style="font-size:18px;color:#009900;"><strong>4.5Mic BOPET film clear transparent , unprinting &amp;&nbsp; uncoating ( for printing and laminating)&nbsp;</strong></span></span></p> <p><span style="color:#FF0000;background-color:rgba(0, 0, 0, 0);"><span style="font-size:18px;color:#009900;"><strong><br/> </strong></span></span></p> <span style="color:#FF0000;background-color:rgba(0, 0, 0, 0);"><span style="font-size:18px;color:#009900;"><strong>The 4.5-Micron (4.5um) clear, transparent BOPET film in its "unprinted and uncoated" state isthe rawest form of high-performance polyester substrate. In the industry, this is often referredto as "Plain Base Film" or "Primary Film." When used specifically for printing andlaminating, this ultra-thin gauge serves as a high-clarity carrier that allows the graphics of thesubstrate beneath it to show through with maximum brilliance.</strong></span><br/> <span style="font-size:18px;color:#009900;"><strong>1. Technical Profile: The "Plain" Base FilmUnlike TTR-grade film (which may have back-coatings) or Metallized film (which has analuminum layer), the Clear/Transparent version focuses on optical purity and surface energy.</strong></span><br/> <span style="font-size:18px;color:#009900;"><strong>2. Why Use "Clear &amp; Uncoated" 4.5um for Lamination?Using a plain 4.5jum film instead of a 12um film or a pre-coated film offers specific industrialadvantages:</strong></span><br/> <span style="font-size:18px;color:#009900;"><strong>A. The "Invisible" Protective ShieldBecause the film is so thin (4.5pm), it is nearly invisible once laminated to paper or cardstock.It provides a high-gloss finish and scratch resistance without the bulk or "plastic feel" of</strong></span><br/> <span style="font-size:18px;color:#009900;"><strong>thicker films.</strong></span><br/> <span style="font-size:18px;color:#009900;"><strong>B. Optimal Thermal Conductivity</strong></span><br/> <span style="font-size:18px;color:#009900;"><strong>In "Thermal Lamination" (where the film is bonded using heat and a thin glue layer), the 4.5umthickness allows heat to pass through almost instantly. This enables faster line speeds onlamination machines compared to thicker substrates.</strong></span><br/> <span style="font-size:18px;color:#009900;"><strong>C. Superior Yield (Area Coverage)</strong></span><br/> <span style="font-size:18px;color:#009900;"><strong>This is the strongest commercial argument.</strong></span><br/> <span style="font-size:18px;color:#009900;"><strong>· 12m PET:Yields ≈ 60 m2/kg.</strong></span><br/> <span style="font-size:18px;color:#009900;"><strong>· 4.5um PET:Yields ≈ 158 m2/kg.</strong></span><br/> <span style="font-size:18px;color:#009900;"><strong>Result: You cover 2.6 times more area with the same weight of plastic, significantlylowering the "cost per square meter."</strong></span><br/> <span style="font-size:18px;color:#009900;"><strong>3. Application Workflow: Printing &amp; Lamination</strong></span><br/> <span style="font-size:18px;color:#009900;"><strong>When a factory buys "Clear Uncoated" 4.5jum film, the process typically follows these steps:1. Reverse Printing (Optional): The film is printed on the "inside" (the side that will beglued). This protects the ink from scratches because it is sandwiched between the filmand the paper.</strong></span><br/> <span style="font-size:18px;color:#009900;"><strong>2. Adhesive Coating: A lamination adhesive (solvent-based or solvent-less) is applied to thetreated side of the 4.5um PET.</strong></span><br/> <span style="font-size:18px;color:#009900;"><strong>3. Nip Rolling (The Lamination): The PET film and the target substrate (e.g., printedcardboard) are pressed together between heated rollers.</strong></span><br/> <span style="font-size:18px;color:#009900;"><strong>4. Curing: The composite is left to dry or cure, creating a permanent bond.</strong></span><br/> <span style="font-size:18px;color:#009900;"><strong>4. Key Performance Keywords for This GradeIf you are sourcing or selling this specific "Clear &amp; Uncoated" grade, use these terms to definethe quality:</strong></span><br/> <span style="font-size:18px;color:#009900;"><strong>&nbsp;Optical Grade: Implies the film is free of "gel" or "fish-eyes" that would ruin the look of aprinted box.</strong></span><br/> <span style="font-size:18px;color:#009900;"><strong>Slip Level: "Medium Slip" is usually preferred so the film can be rewound easily withoutsticking to itself (blocking).</strong></span><br/> <span style="font-size:18px;color:#009900;"><strong>Antistatic Properties: Plain film can be very static-prone; specify "Anti-static treated" ifusing high-speed automatic feeders.</strong></span><br/> <span style="font-size:18px;color:#009900;"><strong>Shrinkage Control: Crucial for lamination; if the film shrinks too much in the dryingtunnel, the final laminated product will "curl."</strong></span><br/> <span style="font-size:18px;color:#009900;"><strong>5. Typical End-Use Products</strong></span><br/> <span style="font-size:18px;color:#009900;"><strong>· Luxury Food Packaging: Glossy boxes for chocolates or tea.</strong></span><br/> <span style="font-size:18px;color:#009900;"><strong>Book Covers &amp; Magazines: High-end dust jackets.</strong></span><br/> <span style="font-size:18px;color:#009900;"><strong>Cosmetic Cartons: Providing a premium, wipe-clean surface.</strong></span><br/> <span style="font-size:18px;color:#009900;"><strong>Security/ldentity Threads: Slit into very narrow strips and embedded into paper.</strong></span><br/> <span style="font-size:18px;color:#009900;"><strong>Would you like me to prepare a "Yield Comparison Chart" for 4.5jm vs. 6jm vs. 12um?This is often the most effective tool for convincing a lamination plant to switch to the thinner</strong></span><br/> <span style="font-size:18px;color:#009900;"><strong>4.5um grade.</strong></span><br/> <br/> <br/> <br/> <br/> </span></p>', '2026-09-06 20:58:45');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO cms_items (c_id, i_id, title, pic, content, addtime) VALUES (66, 429, '4.5 Microns BOPET Film silver metallized', '../upload/image/20211226/20211226095158_77906.jpg', '<p><h3 style="font-family:&quot;color:red;background-color:#FFFFFF;"> <span style="font-size:16px;color:#009900;"><span style="font-family:Tahoma;color:#009900;"> <p class="MsoNormal"> <b>4.5 Micron Silver Metallized BOPET</b></p> <p class="MsoNormal"> <b><br/> </b></p> <p class="MsoNormal"> <b></b></p> <p class="MsoNormal"> <b>4.5 Micron Silver Metallized BOPET Film</b>, this material represents the high-performance intersection of ultra-thin gauge technology and advanced vacuum deposition. At 4.5 microns, it offers a high yield per kilogram, making it exceptionally cost-effective for large-scale industrial applications.</p> <p class="MsoNormal"> <b></b></p> <p class="MsoNormal"> <b>1. Enhanced Barrier Performance</b></p> <p class="MsoNormal"> While the base BOPET provides structural integrity, the vacuum-deposited aluminum layer (typically Ångströms thick) creates a "tortuous path" for molecules.</p> <ul> <li class="MsoNormal"> <b>Light Barrier:</b> 100% opacity in the UV and visible spectrum, preventing degradation of light-sensitive contents.', '2026-09-06 20:58:45');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO cms_items (c_id, i_id, title, pic, content, addtime) VALUES (66, 453, 'BOPET Thermal Transfer Ribbon (TTR) Film 4.5Microns', '../upload/image/20250214/20250214084240_49405.jpg', '<p><span style="font-size:18px;color:#009900;font-family:Verdana;"><strong>B</strong><strong>OPET Thermal Transfer Ribbon (TTR) Film 4.5Microns</strong></span></p> <p><span style="font-size:18px;color:#009900;font-family:Verdana;"><strong><br/> </strong></span></p> <span style="font-size:18px;color:#009900;font-family:Verdana;"><strong> 4.5 microns BOPET Thermal Transfer Ribbon (TTR) Film is an ultra-thin, high-performance material specifically designed for thermal transfer printing applications. It is widely</strong><strong> used in industries requiring high-resolution, durable, and efficient printing, such as barcode labeling, product identification, and industrial marking. Below is a detailed introduction to this specialized film:</strong></span><br/> <span style="font-size:18px;color:#009900;font-family:Verdana;"><strong> 1. Material Overview</strong></span><br/> <span style="font-size:18px;color:#009900;font-family:Verdana;"><strong> Thickness: 4.5 microns (µm), making it an ultra-thin film.</strong></span><br/> <span style="font-size:18px;color:#009900;font-family:Verdana;"><strong> Base Material: Biaxially Oriented Polyethylene Terephthalate (BOPET), a type of polyester film known for its strength, thermal stability, and dimensional stability.</strong></span><br/> <span style="font-size:18px;color:#009900;font-family:Verdana;"><strong> Structure: Typically consists of three layers:</strong></span><br/> <span style="font-size:18px;color:#009900;font-family:Verdana;"><strong> Base Film: The BOPET layer provides mechanical support and stability.</strong></span><br/> <span style="font-size:18px;color:#009900;font-family:Verdana;"><strong> Ink Layer: A thermally sensitive coating containing wax, resin, or a hybrid formulation, which transfers onto the substrate during printing.</strong></span><br/> <span style="font-size:18px;color:#009900;font-family:Verdana;"><strong> Back Coating: A protective layer that reduces friction, prevents static buildup, and protects the print head from wear and tear.</strong></span><br/> <span style="font-size:18px;color:#009900;font-family:Verdana;"><strong> 2. Key Features</strong></span><br/> <span style="font-size:18px;color:#009900;font-family:Verdana;"><strong> High Resolution: The 4.5-micron thickness allows for precise and sharp printing, ideal for detailed barcodes, text, and graphics.</strong></span><br/> <span style="font-size:18px;color:#009900;font-family:Verdana;"><strong> Durability: Printed images are resistant to abrasion, chemicals, and environmental factors, ensuring long-lasting legibility.</strong></span><br/> <span style="font-size:18px;color:#009900;font-family:Verdana;"><strong> Efficiency: The ultra-thin design reduces material usage, lowering costs while maintaining high print quality.</strong></span><br/> <span style="font-size:18px;color:#009900;font-family:Verdana;"><strong> Compatibility: Suitable for a wide range of substrates, including paper, synthetic labels, and textiles.</strong></span><br/> <span style="font-size:18px;color:#009900;font-family:Verdana;"><strong> Thermal Stability: Withstands high temperatures during the thermal transfer process without deformation.</strong></span><br/> <span style="font-size:18px;color:#009900;font-family:Verdana;"><strong> 3. Applications</strong></span><br/> <span style="font-size:18px;color:#009900;font-family:Verdana;"><strong> 4.5 microns BOPET TTR film is widely used in industries that require high-quality, durable printing:</strong></span><br/> <span style="font-size:18px;color:#009900;font-family:Verdana;"><strong> Barcode Printing: For retail, logistics, and inventory management systems.</strong></span><br/> <span style="font-size:18px;color:#009900;font-family:Verdana;"><strong> Product Labeling: For consumer goods, pharmaceuticals, and industrial products.</strong></span><br/> <span style="font-size:18px;color:#009900;font-family:Verdana;"><strong> Asset Tagging: For tracking and identification in manufacturing and healthcare.</strong></span><br/> <span style="font-size:18px;color:#009900;font-family:Verdana;"><strong> Specialized Printing: For applications requiring high durability and precision, such as electronics and automotive parts labeling.</strong></span><br/> <span style="font-size:18px;color:#009900;font-family:Verdana;"><strong> 4. Manufacturing Process</strong></span><br/> <span style="font-size:18px;color:#009900;font-family:Verdana;"><strong> Base Film Production: High-quality BOPET film is produced through biaxial orientation, enhancing its strength and thermal stability.</strong></span><br/> <span style="font-size:18px;color:#009900;font-family:Verdana;"><strong> Coating Application: The ink layer is uniformly applied to the base film using advanced coating technologies.</strong></span><br/> <span style="font-size:18px;color:#009900;font-family:Verdana;"><strong> Back Coating: A protective layer is added to the opposite side of the base film to ensure smooth operation and print head protection.</strong></span><br/> <span style="font-size:18px;color:#009900;font-family:Verdana;"><strong> Slitting and Packaging: The film is precision-slitted to the required width and packaged for distribution.</strong></span><br/> <span style="font-size:18px;color:#009900;font-family:Verdana;"><strong> 5. Advantages and Challenges</strong></span><br/> <span style="font-size:18px;color:#009900;font-family:Verdana;"><strong> Advantages:</strong></span><br/> <span style="font-size:18px;color:#009900;font-family:Verdana;"><strong> Ultra-thin and lightweight, reducing material costs.</strong></span><br/> <span style="font-size:18px;color:#009900;font-family:Verdana;"><strong> High print quality with excellent resolution.</strong></span><br/> <span style="font-size:18px;color:#009900;font-family:Verdana;"><strong> Durable and resistant to environmental factors.</strong></span><br/> <span style="font-size:18px;color:#009900;font-family:Verdana;"><strong> Compatible with a wide range of thermal transfer printers.</strong></span><br/> <span style="font-size:18px;color:#009900;font-family:Verdana;"><strong> Challenges:</strong></span><br/> <span style="font-size:18px;color:#009900;font-family:Verdana;"><strong> Requires advanced manufacturing techniques to ensure uniformity and quality.</strong></span><br/> <span style="font-size:18px;color:#009900;font-family:Verdana;"><strong> Sensitive to handling and storage conditions due to its thinness.</strong></span><br/> <span style="font-size:18px;color:#009900;font-family:Verdana;"><strong> 6. Market Outlook</strong></span><br/> <p><span style="font-size:18px;color:#009900;font-family:Verdana;"><strong>The demand for 4.5 microns BOPET TTR film is growing due to the increasing adoption of barcode technology, automation in logistics, and the need for high-quality labeling in various industries. Its superior performance and cost-effectiveness make it a preferred choice for thermal transfer printing applications.</strong></span></p> <p><br/></p> <p><img src="../upload/image/no-photo.png" alt="" /></p> <p><img src="../upload/image/no-photo.png" alt="" /></p>', '2026-09-06 20:58:45');